Information Trajectory Optimization (ITO)

A First-Principles Taxonomy of Computational Operations for Efficient Information Retrieval and Knowledge Systems

Version 0.4 — Validated Theoretical Draft

Epistemic status: Literature-anchored theoretical framework. No original experiments, training runs, benchmarks, or physical deployments have been conducted by the authors. All quantitative values are labelled according to the discipline defined in §14. All proposals are validated through internet literature search and LLM analysis only.

Authors: Ouadi Maakoul + AI Collaborative Research

---

Abstract

Modern information retrieval and retrieval-augmented generation systems are composed of increasingly complex computational pipelines. Despite substantial progress in retrieval quality, generation quality, adaptive retrieval, indexing, reranking, compression, and verification, existing architectures are generally evaluated as complete systems rather than decomposed into reusable computational operations.

This work proposes Information Trajectory Optimization (ITO), a first-principles framework for decomposing heterogeneous information-access architectures into elementary operations, measuring their information contribution and multidimensional computational cost, identifying architectural redundancies and dependencies, and ultimately optimizing information trajectories rather than optimizing entire architectures as indivisible objects.

The central hypothesis is that many information-seeking tasks can be solved through substantially shorter computational trajectories than those implemented by full architectures, while preserving an acceptable level of answer quality, evidence quality, or decision quality.

ITO does not initially propose another retrieval architecture. Instead, it constructs an architecture × operation representation of existing systems and progressively refines the operation taxonomy through contact with real architectures.

Five architectures have so far been used as structural tests: BM25, Naive RAG, Self-RAG, DPR, and ColBERT. These tests have produced several refinements:

· Dense retrieval cannot be represented by a single cost value — its cost depends strongly on index configuration, dataset, and hardware.
· Self-RAG demonstrates that retrieval gating and candidate selection must be distinguished from conventional retrieval and from stopping.
· DPR reveals the need to separate representation construction from similarity search, and to distinguish integrated top-k selection from an independent candidate-selection operation.
· ColBERT validates the decomposition of late interaction into token-level representation, storage, MaxSim interaction, and token pruning — and establishes that MaxSim belongs to retrieval itself, not to post-retrieval reranking.

The resulting framework represents computational cost as a vector conditioned on configuration, data distribution, and execution environment:

\mathbf{C}_i(\theta, D, H) = (C_{\text{compute}}, C_{\text{latency}}, C_{\text{tokens}}, C_{\text{memory}}, C_{\text{network}}, C_{\text{calls}})

with explicit separation between offline and online cost, a per-dimension composition operator, a dependency-aware feasible trajectory space, and a strict convention that absence of measurement is never interpreted as zero cost.

The framework deliberately does not claim that any operation is globally optimal. Conditional information gain remains an empirical quantity to be measured through ablation, controlled intervention, and eventually dependency-aware Shapley-style attribution.

The long-term objective is a general theory for selecting the smallest sufficient information trajectory for a query under explicit quality and resource constraints.

---

1. Problem Statement

Modern knowledge systems increasingly combine lexical retrieval, dense retrieval, late-interaction retrieval, query transformation, reranking, filtering, graph traversal, evidence verification, adaptive retrieval, context compression, generation, answer verification, provenance tracking, memory, and stopping policies.

These mechanisms are usually presented as architectures:

\text{Query} \rightarrow \text{Retriever} \rightarrow \text{Reranker} \rightarrow \text{Generator}

or:

\text{Query} \rightarrow \text{Retrieve} \rightarrow \text{Reflect} \rightarrow \text{Retrieve again} \rightarrow \text{Generate}

The difficulty is that two architectures may contain many of the same computational functions while arranging them differently. Consequently, comparing architectures as indivisible objects can hide the actual source of computational cost and informational benefit.

ITO asks a different question:

What elementary operations does an information system actually perform, what does each operation cost, what information does it contribute conditional on the state already reached, and can the same decision quality be obtained through a shorter trajectory?

1.1 Related Work Positioning

ITO is not the first framework to decompose retrieval systems. Existing taxonomies organise design choices across architectural layers — for example, Representation, Granularity, Orchestration, and Robustness. Such taxonomies are complementary to ITO: they describe what a system is composed of, whereas ITO describes what it costs and how little of it is sufficient.

Dimension Existing taxonomies ITO
Decomposition level Architectural layers Computational primitives
Cost model Not specified Vector-valued, configuration-conditioned
Quality model Not specified Vector-valued, task-specific
Optimization object Design choices Trajectories
Empirical protocol Not specified Controlled ablation + dependency-aware attribution

A recent SoK formalises agentic RAG loops as finite-horizon POMDPs with explicit control policies. ITO's contribution is not the POMDP formulation itself but the operation-level decomposition that makes the state space, action space, and cost function measurable.

---

2. The Fundamental Object: An Information Trajectory

Let a query be $q \in \mathcal{Q}$, and let a computational trajectory be

p = (o_1, o_2, \ldots, o_n)

where each $o_i$ is an operation from a finite or progressively refined operation set $\mathcal{O} = \{O_1, O_2, \ldots, O_m\}$.

The trajectory transforms an initial information state $S_0$ into successive states:

S_0 \xrightarrow{o_1} S_1 \xrightarrow{o_2} S_2 \rightarrow \cdots \xrightarrow{o_n} S_n.

The final state is used to produce an answer, decision, retrieval result, or other task output.

ITO therefore treats computation not simply as a collection of modules, but as a path through an information state space.

2.1 Explicit State Definition

The information state $S$ is defined explicitly as a tuple:

\boxed{S = (Q_{\text{retrieved}}, Q_{\text{compressed}}, \text{budget\_consumed}, \text{verification\_flags})}

where:

· $Q_{\text{retrieved}}$ = the set of retrieved evidence items,
· $Q_{\text{compressed}}$ = the compressed context derived from retrieved evidence,
· $\text{budget\_consumed}$ = the vector of resources consumed so far,
· $\text{verification\_flags}$ = the set of epistemic flags raised by verification operations.

This definition makes conditional information gain $G_i(S) = \Delta Q(O_i \mid S)$ falsifiable and measurable in principle. It aligns with the POMDP belief state formalized in the SoK on agentic RAG.

---

3. The Architecture × Operation Matrix

The first methodological instrument is an architecture–operation presence matrix:

P_{ji} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if architecture } A_j \text{ contains operation } O_i \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

This matrix is explicitly not the final optimization model. It is a microscope. It allows us to identify common operations, architecture-specific operations, apparent redundancies, missing operations, structural invariants, dependencies, and candidate substitution points.

The matrix must therefore be supplemented by at least four additional objects:

P, \quad C, \quad G, \quad D

where:

· $P$ = presence,
· $C$ = cost (vector-valued, configuration-conditioned),
· $G$ = conditional information/quality gain,
· $D$ = dependency structure (DAG).

3.1 Functional Operation vs Independent Execution Primitive

A critical methodological distinction emerges from the DPR and Self-RAG analyses:

An operation may exist conceptually without appearing as a separately measurable computational node.

For example, DPR performs top-k selection, so candidate selection functionally exists. But in DPR's actual implementation, top-k selection is integrated into the similarity search itself, not implemented as an independent module.

ITO therefore distinguishes:

· Functional operations — what the system accomplishes as a whole.
· Independent execution primitives — what can be separately measured, ablated, and assigned a distinct cost.

Only the second category earns a column in the experimental matrix. The first category may still be tracked for conceptual clarity.

3.2 Layered Operation Registry (L0/L1/L2)

Following validated critical advice, the operation registry is organized into three layers:

· L0 — Primitives: Operations that can in principle be measured with the instruments defined in §4.2. If an operation cannot be profiled with a declared instrument, it does not receive an L0 ID.
· L1 — Families: Composite operations such as $O_7 = \{O_{7a}, O_{7b}, O_{7c}\}$ and $O_8 = \{O_{8a}, O_{8b}, O_{8c}, O_{8d}\}$.
· L2 — Patterns: Meta-taxonomic categories such as compression, routing, and verification. No cost is assigned at this layer.

Rule: Do not assign an ID to a hypothesis. Hypotheses are labeled [H] and tracked separately.

---

4. Cost Must Be a Vector

A central result emerging from the DPR and ColBERT analyses is that an operation does not possess one universal cost.

\mathbf{C}_i = (C_{\text{compute}}, C_{\text{latency}}, C_{\text{tokens}}, C_{\text{memory}}, C_{\text{network}}, C_{\text{calls}}).

Furthermore:

\mathbf{C}_i = \mathbf{C}_i(\theta, D, H)

where:

· $\theta$ = algorithmic / index configuration,
· $D$ = dataset and query distribution,
· $H$ = hardware / execution environment.

This distinction is essential. Dense retrieval using a flat index and dense retrieval using an approximate-nearest-neighbour structure can implement essentially the same semantic retrieval operation while exhibiting radically different online latency and memory requirements.

Therefore:

\boxed{C(O_7) \neq \text{constant}}

and more appropriately:

\boxed{C(O_7 \mid \theta_{\text{index}}, D, H)}

must be measured.

4.1 NR ≠ 0

If a publication does not report a specific cost dimension (e.g., memory), ITO records:

\boxed{\text{NR}}

— not $0$. Absence of measurement is not evidence of zero cost. NR is not imputed in the optimization layer.

4.2 Measurement Instruments

Each cost dimension requires an explicit measurement instrument:

Dimension Unit Instrument
$C_{\text{compute}}$ FLOPs or GPU-seconds Hardware profiler
$C_{\text{latency}}$ milliseconds Wall-clock timer
$C_{\text{tokens}}$ count Tokenizer
$C_{\text{memory}}$ GB Memory profiler
$C_{\text{network}}$ bytes or requests Network monitor
$C_{\text{calls}}$ count Instrumentation

$C_{\text{calls}}$ counts discrete invocation events that incur fixed overhead: API calls, model forward passes, network requests.

4.3 Per-Dimension Cost Composition Rules

The trajectory cost is defined via a composition operator $\oplus$:

\mathbf{C}(p) = \bigoplus_{o_i \in p} \mathbf{C}_i(\theta_i, D, H).

The composition operator must be specified per dimension, because different physical quantities compose differently:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
C_{\text{latency}}(p) &= \begin{cases} \max_i C_{\text{latency}}(o_i) & \text{under pipelining} \\ \sum_i C_{\text{latency}}(o_i) & \text{under sequential execution} \end{cases} \\
C_{\text{memory}}(p) &= \begin{cases} \max_i C_{\text{memory}}(o_i) & \text{under shared pool} \\ \sum_i C_{\text{memory}}(o_i) & \text{under isolated execution} \end{cases} \\
C_{\text{tokens}}(p) &= \sum_i C_{\text{tokens}}(o_i) \\
C_{\text{calls}}(p) &= \sum_i C_{\text{calls}}(o_i) \\
C_{\text{compute}}(p) &= \text{model-dependent (e.g., Roofline)} \\
C_{\text{network}}(p) &= \text{model-dependent}
\end{aligned}
}

The additive form $\sum$ is a first-order approximation valid when operations are resource-independent and sequentially executed. The general operator is $\oplus$, with the above rules specifying its instantiation per dimension.

---

5. Offline Cost Versus Online Cost

ITO explicitly separates:

\mathbf{C}_i = \mathbf{C}_i^{\text{offline}} + \mathbf{C}_i^{\text{online}}

although the dimensions themselves should not be added numerically without explicit deployment weights, because they represent different physical/resource quantities.

Offline cost may include document encoding, index construction, model preparation, token representation generation, storage construction, pruning, and graph construction.

Online cost may include query encoding, retrieval, similarity computation, reranking, generation, verification, network calls, and additional retrieval iterations.

This distinction became unavoidable in the DPR and ColBERT analyses. An expensive offline computation can reduce online latency.

5.1 Principle: Optimization as Temporal Redistribution

This suggests a general ITO principle:

\boxed{\text{Optimization can be temporal redistribution of computational cost.}}

For example:

C_{\text{offline}} \uparrow \quad\text{while}\quad C_{\text{online}} \downarrow.

The framework is therefore not simply about minimizing cost, but about choosing where in time cost is paid subject to deployment constraints.

---

6. Information Gain Cannot Yet Be Assumed

A preliminary efficiency concept is:

\eta_i = \frac{\Delta I_i}{C_i}.

This formulation is incomplete for two reasons.

First, the benefit of an operation depends on what has already been performed:

G_i(S) = \Delta Q(O_i \mid S)

where $S$ is the current information state as defined in §2.1. Therefore:

G_i(S_1) \neq G_i(S_2)

in general. An operation may be highly useful early in a trajectory and nearly useless after another operation has already extracted the relevant information.

Second, vector division is not mathematically defined in the ordinary scalar sense. The scalar $\eta_i$ must be replaced by a quality–cost response vector, or converted into a scalar only after deployment weights are explicitly specified.

---

7. Why Operation Ordering Matters

Consider:

O_a \rightarrow O_b \quad\text{versus}\quad O_b \rightarrow O_a.

The marginal contribution of $O_b$ can differ between the two trajectories:

\Delta Q_b \mid O_a \neq \Delta Q_b \mid \varnothing.

ITO therefore cannot assign universal values such as "O7 gives 0.4 information units" without specifying the state in which O7 is executed.

7.1 Dependency Structure $D$

The dependency structure is a directed acyclic graph $D = (V, E)$ where $V = \mathcal{O}$ and edges have types:

\boxed{E \subseteq V \times V \times \{\text{requires}, \text{conditions}, \text{mutually\_excludes}\}}

· requires: $O_j$ cannot execute without $O_i$. Example: $O_{7c}$ requires $O_{7a}$ and $O_{7b}$.
· conditions: $O_i$ modifies the cost or behavior of $O_j$ but is not a prerequisite. Example: $O_{30}$ (Retrieval Gate) conditions $O_7$.
· mutually_excludes: $O_i$ and $O_j$ cannot both appear in a feasible trajectory.

Feasible trajectory space: The feasible trajectory space $\mathcal{P}(q)$ is the set of topological orderings of $D$ compatible with the query, not the full powerset of $\mathcal{O}$.

Example DAG — ColBERT-style late interaction:

```
OFFLINE
  O8a Token Encoding ──► O8b Token Storage
                              │
                              ▼
                        Θ_index (parameter space)

ONLINE
  Query ──► O7a Query Encoding ──► O8c MaxSim Interaction ──► O31 Candidate Selection
                                                                      │
                                                                      ▼
                                                                O24 Generation
```

Edges:

· $O_{8a} \xrightarrow{\text{requires}} O_{8b}$
· $O_{8b} \xrightarrow{\text{conditions}} O_{8c}$ (via $\Theta_{\text{index}}$)
· $O_{7a} \xrightarrow{\text{requires}} O_{8c}$
· $O_{8c} \xrightarrow{\text{requires}} O_{31}$
· $O_{31} \xrightarrow{\text{requires}} O_{24}$
· $O_{8d} \xrightarrow{\text{conditions}} O_{8b}$ (pruning reduces storage)

7.2 Three Empirical Approaches

· A. Canonical trajectories. Define standard architecture-specific orders. Simple, but biased by the choice of order.
· B. Dependency-aware Shapley attribution. Sample feasible permutations (respecting $D$) of operations and estimate average marginal contributions. Non-biased but costly. Prior work on Shapley Context Pruning provides Monte Carlo sample-complexity bounds for the closely related problem of context-unit attribution; ITO extends this to operation-level attribution with the caveat that the standard permutation-invariance assumption is violated by $D$.
· C. Controlled ablation. Remove one operation or operation class while keeping other variables fixed. Approximate but an order of magnitude cheaper than Shapley.

For the first empirical phase, controlled ablation (C) with query stratification is the cleanest starting point. Dependency-aware Shapley (B) can subsequently be applied to a smaller set of promising operations identified by (C).

---

8. The Experimental Hypothesis

The central ITO hypothesis is:

\boxed{\exists p \in \mathcal{P}(q) : \operatorname{Cost}(p) \ll \operatorname{Cost}(p_{\text{full}})}

while:

\boxed{Q(p) \approx Q(p_{\text{full}})}

where $p$ is a reduced trajectory, $p_{\text{full}}$ is the reference architecture, and $Q$ is task-specific quality.

This is a hypothesis. It is not yet a result.

The null hypothesis is:

H_0 : \text{decomposition does not improve the Pareto frontier.}

The alternative is:

H_1 : \text{decomposition enables a less costly trajectory at equivalent quality.}

This formulation is falsifiable.

---

9. The Five Architectural Tests

ITO has so far been stress-tested structurally against:

1. BM25 — lexical retrieval baseline,
2. Naive RAG — minimal retrieval-to-generation pipeline,
3. Self-RAG — conditional retrieval and reflection,
4. DPR — dense bi-encoder retrieval,
5. ColBERT — late-interaction retrieval.

The purpose was not to determine which architecture is superior. The purpose was to ask: Can the proposed operations survive contact with fundamentally different retrieval paradigms?

The taxonomy has changed after each test. That refinement is itself evidence that the methodology is useful, but it is not evidence that the final taxonomy is correct.

---

10. BM25: Lexical Retrieval as the Baseline

BM25 originates within the probabilistic relevance framework and became a standard lexical ranking function in information retrieval.

A simplified BM25 trajectory is:

```
QUERY
  │
  ▼
O5 Lexical Retrieval
  │
  ▼
Ranked Documents
```

Status. BM25 is a retrieval model, not a complete RAG architecture. It is retained in the matrix as a primitive baseline, not as a comparable architecture.

BM25 demonstrates that a retrieval system does not necessarily require neural query encoding, dense representations, late interaction, generation, adaptive retrieval, or multi-hop control.

Operation presence. Only $O_5 = 1$; all other operations $= 0$. Query parsing is not counted as $O_1$ merely because an inverted index tokenises the query — that would artificially upgrade a mechanical step into a modelled operation.

---

11. Naive RAG: The Minimal Trajectory

A minimal retrieval-augmented generation architecture can be represented as:

```
QUERY
  │
  ▼
O7 Dense Retrieval
  │
  ▼
Retrieved Passages
  │
  ▼
O24 Generation
  │
  ▼
ANSWER
```

Status. Naive RAG establishes a useful baseline containing $O_7 + O_{24}$. It does not demonstrate that additional verification, reranking, decomposition, or adaptive control is always necessary.

Cost structure. Online cost decomposes as:

C_{\text{RAG}} = C_{\text{embed}} + C_{\text{retrieve}} + C_{\text{LLM}}

with offline cost corresponding primarily to document encoding and index construction.

---

12. Self-RAG: Retrieval Becomes Conditional

Self-RAG introduced adaptive retrieval and reflection mechanisms within a single language-model framework. A simplified trajectory is:

```
QUERY
  │
  ▼
O30 Retrieval Gate
  ├── NO  ──────────────► GENERATION
  └── YES
        │
        ▼
      O7 Retrieval
        │
        ▼
      Retrieved Evidence
        │
        ▼
      Evidence Verification (O15)
        │
        ▼
      Generation (O24)
        │
        ▼
      Answer Verification (O27)
        ├── sufficient ──► STOP
        └── insufficient ► further retrieval
```

12.1 Taxonomic Refinements from Self-RAG

O30: Retrieval Gate. This operation decides whether retrieval should begin. It is not equivalent to STOP.

O21: STOP. STOP terminates an active trajectory.

\boxed{\text{Retrieval Gate} \neq \text{STOP}}

Self-RAG also motivates explicit representation of answer verification and evidence verification.

12.2 Sufficiency Estimation vs STOP

Following validated critical advice, $O_{20}$ (Sufficiency Estimation) and $O_{21}$ (STOP) are distinct primitives:

· $O_{20}$ — Sufficiency Estimation: Determines whether the current state contains sufficient information to answer.
· $O_{21}$ — STOP: Terminates the trajectory. May be triggered by sufficiency, by budget exhaustion, or by a training-free predicate.

TASR implements STOP as a training-free predicate based on answer stability; S2G-RAG implements sufficiency as a trained structured judge. These are different computational primitives with different cost profiles and failure modes.

Theorem (Cost of Reduced Retrieval): Reduced retrieval calls do not imply reduced total compute:

\boxed{C_{\text{total}} = C_{\text{retrieval\_saved}} + C_{\text{judge}}}

The judge itself consumes compute; the net effect depends on the relative magnitudes.

12.3 Implementation Note

In Self-RAG, the concepts ISREL, ISSUP, ISUSE, and retrieval decision are implemented as reflection tokens produced by the same model. They are not necessarily independent modules. This validates the rule that the matrix must record the implementation mechanism, not merely the functional presence.

Self-RAG therefore motivates an additional primitive:

\boxed{O_{31} = \text{Candidate Selection}}

when a mechanism explicitly selects a subset from an already-produced candidate set, distinctly from scoring or filtering. Status: [H] — functional existence unresolved.

---

13. DPR: The First Major Refinement of O7

Dense Passage Retrieval introduced a dense bi-encoder architecture for open-domain question answering. The fundamental transformation is:

q \xrightarrow{E_Q} \mathbf{q}, \qquad d_i \xrightarrow{E_D} \mathbf{d}_i.

Similarity is computed through:

s(q, d_i) = \mathbf{q}^\top \mathbf{d}_i.

The top candidates are retrieved according to this similarity.

---

14. DPR Demonstrates That O7 Is a Family

The initial taxonomy treated $O_7$ as Dense Retrieval. DPR demonstrates that this is too coarse. At minimum:

· $O_{7a}$ — Query Encoding: $q \rightarrow \mathbf{q}$
· $O_{7b}$ — Document Encoding: $d_i \rightarrow \mathbf{d}_i$
· $O_{7c}$ — Similarity Search: $\mathbf{q}, \{\mathbf{d}_i\} \rightarrow \{s_i\}$

```
OFFLINE
Corpus
  │
  ▼
O7b Document Encoding
  │
  ▼
Document Representations
  │
  ▼
Θ_index (Index Configuration)
  │
  ▼
Search Index

ONLINE
Query
  │
  ▼
O7a Query Encoding
  │
  ▼
O7c Similarity Search
  │
  ▼
Top-k passages
```

This is a major refinement because the three components have different temporal and computational properties. $O_{7b}$ is offline; $O_{7a}$ and $O_{7c}$ are online.

---

15. DPR and the O31 Candidate Selection Question

The top-k mechanism creates an important taxonomic ambiguity. One could represent DPR as $O_7 \rightarrow O_{31}$ where $O_{31}$ selects the final candidates. But in the actual DPR implementation, top-k selection is integrated into the retrieval/search process.

Therefore the correct conclusion is not:

DPR proves that $O_{31}$ is an independent module.

Instead:

\boxed{\text{DPR demonstrates the functional existence of candidate selection,}}

but does not yet demonstrate that candidate selection must be represented as an independent execution primitive. This distinction is crucial, and ITO must preserve it in all future analyses. $O_{31}$ is currently classified as [H] — functional operation, independence unresolved.

---

16. DPR and Index Configuration

Large-scale dense retrieval makes index structure a major determinant of system cost. A controlled comparison on 21M documents reports the following configuration-specific measurements:

Configuration Build time Index size Retrieval latency NQ Top-5
BM25 0.5 h 2.4 GB 0.15 s 43.8
DPR standard 3.5 h 64 GB 22.4 s 68.3
DPR + HNSW 8.5 h 142 GB 0.04 s 68.0

Epistemic status: [V]. These values are directly reported by the cited source under specified experimental conditions. They are configuration-specific measurements, not universal constants for BM25 or DPR, and must not be transferred to other hardware, corpus sizes, or implementations without explicit re-measurement.

Note: This table is presented as a configuration-specific illustration, not as a validated point estimate. For the Pareto frontier, a $C(\theta)$ curve should be derived rather than a single point.

The result demonstrates the structural principle:

\boxed{C(O_7) = C(O_7 \mid \theta_{\text{index}}, D, H)}

rather than $C(O_7) = \text{constant}$.

---

17. Index Configuration as Parameter Space Θ_index

Following validated critical advice, index configuration is not an operation with its own cost. It is a parameter space:

\boxed{\Theta_{\text{index}} = \{\text{Flat}, \text{HNSW}, \text{IVF}, \text{PQ}, \ldots\}}

that conditions the cost function of retrieval operations. The composition operator $\oplus$ is defined over operations, not configurations.

```
INDEX DESIGN
  │
  ▼
Θ_index (Parameter Space)
  ╱          ╲
Flat         HNSW
  ╲          ╱
   ▼        ▼
   O7
   │
 ONLINE
   ▼
Retrieval
```

ITO principle: An operation is not fully characterised without its implementation configuration. Configuration has no direct cost; it only conditions cost.

---

18. ColBERT: The Second Major Stress Test

ColBERT introduces a fundamentally different retrieval mechanism. Instead of representing each document using a single vector, ColBERT represents documents and queries at token level and performs a late interaction using MaxSim. The central score is:

S(Q, D) = \sum_{i=1}^{L_Q} \max_{j=1}^{L_D} \langle Q_i, D_j \rangle.

The original ColBERT paper explicitly positions late interaction as an alternative between expensive cross-encoding and cheaper single-vector retrieval, while allowing document representations to be computed offline. This provides a direct test of whether $O_8$ should remain a single operation. It should not.

---

19. O8: Late Interaction as a Composite Family

ITO now decomposes $O_8$ into:

\boxed{O_8 = \{O_{8a}, O_{8b}, O_{8c}, O_{8d}\}}

· $O_{8a}$ — Token Encoding. Each document token is transformed into a vector representation: $d \rightarrow (D_1, \ldots, D_{L_D})$.
· $O_{8b}$ — Token Representation Storage. Unlike DPR ($1 \text{ doc} \rightarrow 1 \text{ vector}$), ColBERT produces $1 \text{ doc} \rightarrow L_D \text{ vectors}$. This produces a fundamentally different memory profile.
· $O_{8c}$ — MaxSim Interaction. For each query token $Q_i$, the system finds $\max_j \langle Q_i, D_j \rangle$ and aggregates the maxima.
· $O_{8d}$ — Token Pruning. The representation set can be reduced: $\{D_1, \ldots, D_L\} \rightarrow \{D_{j_1}, \ldots, D_{j_m}\}$ with $m < L$, while attempting to preserve retrieval quality.

---

20. Why MaxSim Is Not Ordinary Reranking

A conventional cross-encoder reranker receives a query–document pair and performs a new joint neural forward pass:

(q, d) \rightarrow \text{cross-encoder} \rightarrow s(q, d).

ColBERT instead uses separately encoded representations and performs late interaction:

Q_i, D_j \rightarrow \operatorname{sim}(Q_i, D_j) \rightarrow \operatorname{MaxSim} \rightarrow S(Q, D).

\boxed{O_{8c} \neq O_{12}}

· $O_{8c}$ = MaxSim late interaction, part of the retrieval mechanism itself.
· $O_{12}$ = reranking through an additional scoring mechanism applied to an existing candidate set.

The original ColBERT paper reports substantially lower computational requirements than BERT-based reranking approaches, reflecting the architectural difference between precomputed document representations and repeated joint encoding. More recent ColBERT analyses have reported millisecond-scale warm MaxSim execution under specific implementations, but such latency values must remain tied to the particular hardware, corpus, index, software implementation, and candidate regime in which they were measured. ITO therefore does not encode any specific MaxSim latency as a universal constant. Instead:

\boxed{C(O_{8c} \mid \theta, D, H, k)}

is the scientifically defensible object.

---

21. O8d: Token Pruning

Token pruning is now an active line of research. The objective is:

\{D_1, \ldots, D_L\} \rightarrow \{D_{j_1}, \ldots, D_{j_m}\}, \quad m < L

while maintaining acceptable retrieval quality.

Published evidence [R]:

· Heuristic pruning (position, IDF, special tokens) supports up to ~30% reduction on MS MARCO passages without significant performance loss.
· Learned pruning via attention-based token selection supports up to ~70% reduction on the same benchmark under different experimental conditions.
· Pruning learned indexes reports reductions of index size with limited quality loss.
· Dynamic pruning of MaxSim computation at run-time has been reported.

Implication. $O_{8d}$ must be represented with an explicit pruning method configuration $\theta_{\text{prune}}$, analogous to index configuration:

\boxed{C(O_{8d} \mid \theta_{\text{prune}}, D)}

rather than a universal pruning ratio.

21.1 Query-Agnostic vs Query-Aware Pruning

Offline pruning $D \rightarrow D'$ occurs before the query exists. Online pruning $(D, q) \rightarrow D'(q)$ depends on the query.

\boxed{O_{8d}^{\text{offline}} \neq O_{8d}^{\text{online}}}

at the cost and dependency level, even though both implement the broader function of token reduction.

---

22. The Meta-Taxonomy of Compression

At first glance, $O_{8d}$ (token pruning) and $O_{14}$ (context compression) appear similar. Both transform $X \rightarrow X'$ such that $|X'| < |X|$ while attempting to preserve decision-relevant information: $Q(X') \approx Q(X)$.

But their locations in the computational pipeline are different:

· $O_{8d}$ acts on retrieval index representations — affects index memory, retrieval computation, retrieval latency.
· $O_{14}$ acts on generator input context — affects context length, generation compute, generation latency, token usage.

Therefore:

\boxed{O_{8d} \neq O_{14}} \quad\text{while}\quad \boxed{O_{8d} \sim O_{14}}

at the level of a higher-order functional pattern: information compression under a task-quality constraint. This is an L2 pattern, not an L0 primitive.

This suggests a future meta-taxonomy of information-preserving compression operations, without merging their architectural identities.

