24. The Sparse/Dense Boundary

BM25, DPR, and ColBERT also demonstrate that retrieval paradigms should not be treated as mutually exclusive alternatives.

· Lexical retrieval provides strong exact-match behaviour.
· Dense retrieval provides semantic matching.
· Late interaction retains finer-grained token-level matching while exploiting neural representations.
· Learned sparse retrieval (e.g., SPLADE) further complicates the boundary.

Therefore $O_5$, $O_7$, and $O_8$ should be treated as distinct retrieval primitives/families rather than mutually exclusive architectural categories.

This has a direct consequence for $O_{30}$.

---

25. Retrieval Gate as a Routing Problem

The retrieval gate should not merely answer "retrieve?" It may eventually need to answer:

\boxed{\text{which retrieval mechanism should be invoked?}}

```
QUERY
  │
  ▼
O30 Retrieval Gate
  ├──────────┬──────────┐
  ▼          ▼          ▼
  O5         O7         O8
lexical     dense      late interaction
  │          │          │
  └──────────┼──────────┘
             ▼
         candidates
```

This is currently a research hypothesis [H], not an established result. The matrix could eventually test whether query characteristics predict which retrieval mechanism provides the best quality–cost tradeoff. A classifier $f(q) \rightarrow \theta_{\text{retrieval}}$ could route queries to the optimal retrieval mechanism.

---

26. The Cost Surface

The emerging framework suggests that ITO should not seek one scalar cost function initially. Instead define:

\mathbf{C}(p) = \bigoplus_{o_i \in p} \mathbf{C}_i(\theta_i, D, H).

A deployment may later define weights:

C_w = w_c C_{\text{compute}} + w_l C_{\text{latency}} + w_t C_{\text{tokens}} + w_m C_{\text{memory}} + w_n C_{\text{network}} + w_a C_{\text{calls}}.

Then:

\eta_w = \frac{\Delta Q}{\Delta C_w}.

But these weights must come from the deployment objective. There is no universal justification for $w_{\text{latency}} = w_{\text{memory}}$ or any other arbitrary equivalence.

26.1 NR Policy Under Constraints

If a cost dimension is NR for an operation, that operation cannot be selected for deployment in contexts where that dimension is constrained. NR is not imputed. This rule preserves the epistemic discipline of §4.1 in the optimization layer.

---

27. Quality Must Also Be Vector-Valued

The same logic applies to quality. Define:

\mathbf{Q} = (Q_{\text{retrieval}}, Q_{\text{answer}}, Q_{\text{factuality}}, Q_{\text{citation}}, Q_{\text{coverage}}, Q_{\text{task}}).

An operation may improve one dimension while leaving another unchanged. For example:

\Delta Q_{\text{retrieval}} > 0 \not\Rightarrow \Delta Q_{\text{answer}} > 0.

Therefore ITO should avoid collapsing quality into a single number until the target task defines the relevant objective.

27.1 Operational Definitions

Each quality dimension requires an explicit measurement instrument:

Dimension Operational definition Instrument
$Q_{\text{retrieval}}$ Recall@k, nDCG@10 Standard IR metrics
$Q_{\text{answer}}$ Exact Match (EM), F1 Standard QA metrics
$Q_{\text{factuality}}$ Proportion of predicted subclaims attested in reference INFOF1
$Q_{\text{citation}}$ Citation support (precision) and citation attribution (recall) at subclaim level CITEF1
$Q_{\text{coverage}}$ Proportion of reference subclaims attested in prediction INFOF1
$Q_{\text{task}}$ Task-specific (e.g., accuracy, F1) Task-dependent

The quality vector is not fully specified until the task and measurement instruments are declared.

---

28. The Core Optimization Problem

ITO can ultimately be formulated as a constrained multi-objective problem. The solution is a Pareto frontier rather than a single globally optimal trajectory.

\boxed{\mathcal{P}^\star(q) = \left\{ p \in \mathcal{P}(q) \;\middle|\; \mathbf{Q}(p) \succeq \boldsymbol{\tau}, \;\nexists p' : \mathbf{C}(p') \preceq \mathbf{C}(p) \text{ with at least one strict inequality} \right\}}

A trajectory $p_1$ dominates $p_2$ if it is no worse in every relevant cost dimension and strictly better in at least one, while satisfying the same quality constraints. Different deployments can legitimately choose different points on the frontier.

Deployment selection. A specific $p^\star \in \mathcal{P}^\star(q)$ is selected by specifying a scalarization $\mathbf{w}$ over cost dimensions and a scalarization over quality dimensions, or by imposing additional constraints.

Why not vector argmin. The expression $\arg\min_{p} \mathbf{C}(p)$ is not defined for vector-valued $\mathbf{C}$ without a scalarization or dominance criterion. ITO therefore adopts the Pareto formulation as the primary object, with scalarization as a deployment-specific post-processing step.

---

29. What We Know After Five Tests

Finding 1 — Architecture decomposition is viable. The operation taxonomy survived five structurally different architectures, although it changed repeatedly.

Finding 2 — Operations cannot be characterised independently of implementation. DPR and ColBERT both demonstrate strong configuration dependence.

Finding 3 — Offline and online cost must be separated. Dense and token-level retrieval can shift substantial computational cost into preprocessing and indexing.

Finding 4 — Retrieval is not a single primitive. Lexical, dense, and late-interaction retrieval operate at different representational granularities.

Finding 5 — Late interaction is structurally distinct from conventional reranking. MaxSim is part of the retrieval mechanism itself.

Finding 6 — Candidate selection and retrieval are not automatically separate execution operations. DPR integrates top-k selection into retrieval.

Finding 7 — Compression is a recurring computational pattern. Token pruning and context compression share a functional objective but operate at different stages.

Finding 8 — Retrieval gating may become a routing problem. The gate may eventually choose not only whether to retrieve but how to retrieve.

Finding 9 — Sufficiency estimation and STOP are distinct primitives. TASR and S2G-RAG implement different mechanisms with different cost profiles.

Finding 10 — Reduced retrieval does not imply reduced total compute. The judge itself consumes compute; net effect depends on relative magnitudes.

---

30. What We Do NOT Know Yet

This section is essential. ITO currently does not establish that:

· fewer operations always produce equivalent answers;
· a particular operation has universally positive information gain;
· $O_5$, $O_7$, or $O_8$ can be selected optimally from query features;
· $O_{31}$ should always be an independent computational primitive;
· MaxSim is universally cheaper than every reranking strategy;
· token pruning preserves quality across all datasets and tasks;
· a particular retrieval trajectory is globally optimal;
· the DAG structure $D$ is stable across architectures;
· the composition operator $\oplus$ is well-approximated by $\sum$ in realistic deployments;
· the retrieval gate routing hypothesis (§25) holds empirically.

These are hypotheses requiring experiments.

---

31. The Next Theoretical Layer

The next architectures should be selected specifically because they attack unresolved portions of the taxonomy.

31.1 GraphRAG / HippoRAG / LineageRAG

Primary tests: $O_{10}$ Graph Retrieval, provenance.

Questions:

· Is graph construction an offline primitive?
· Is graph traversal retrieval or reasoning?
· Is provenance an independent operation or metadata attached to evidence?
· Can graph retrieval be decomposed into node selection, edge traversal, subgraph extraction, and evidence aggregation?

Literature anchor. LineageRAG constructs one evidence lineage for each query-derived evidence demand and completes it with a verbatim source span. The key architectural insight is that graph-based retrieval finds relevant candidates, but the final evidence set does not record the requirement behind each source; this missing connection can leave GraphRAG with an incomplete evidence chain. HippoRAG propagates relevance across an OpenIE graph using Personalized PageRank.

Theoretical implication. Provenance may be an independent computational operation in LineageRAG. Graph construction is offline; traversal is online. Graph traversal may be retrieval, not reasoning. The DAG for GraphRAG-like systems therefore includes a directed edge from offline graph construction to online traversal, with provenance tracking as a separate online primitive.

31.2 TASR / S2G-RAG

Primary tests: $O_{20}$ Sufficiency, $O_{21}$ STOP.

Questions:

· Is sufficiency estimation independent from novelty detection?
· Does sufficiency change the retrieval trajectory?
· Can stopping be represented as an optimisation decision?
· What measurable marginal information gain justifies another retrieval step?
· Are training-free and trained stopping mechanisms distinct primitives?

Literature anchor. TASR is a training-free stopping rule that fires when the model repeats its previous-round normalized answer and the isotonically calibrated logit margin exceeds a fixed threshold. S2G-RAG asks a structured judge to predict both sufficiency and the missing information needed for the next retrieval. On HotpotQA, a trained S2G-style structured judge reduces retrieval calls by 77 (3.70%) relative to a reference baseline, while Exact Match decreases by 0.625 percentage points.

Theoretical implication. $O_{20}$ (Sufficiency Estimation) and $O_{21}$ (STOP) are distinct primitives. The 77-call reduction and the 0.625-point EM decrease together provide a concrete quality–cost data point. Critically, reduced retrieval does not automatically imply reduced total inference cost, because the judge itself consumes compute. This motivates the cost-of-reduced-retrieval theorem in §12.2.

31.3 Missing Operations to Investigate

The following operations are present in the literature but not yet instantiated in the ITO taxonomy:

· Query Decomposition / Rewriting — present in agentic RAG planning mechanisms.
· Relevance Filtering — distinct from $O_{12}$ reranking and $O_{31}$ selection.
· Provenance Linking — may include verbatim span extraction cost.

These should be tested against the layered registry in v0.5.

