32. Proposed Experimental Protocol

Before attempting a full POMDP formulation, ITO should perform controlled experiments.

For a selected architecture, define:

B = \text{minimal baseline}.

Then progressively add operations:

B + O_i, \qquad B + O_i + O_j, \qquad \ldots

Measure:

\Delta Q = Q_{\text{augmented}} - Q_{\text{baseline}}, \qquad \Delta \mathbf{C} = \mathbf{C}_{\text{augmented}} - \mathbf{C}_{\text{baseline}}.

The resulting empirical quantity is:

\boxed{\boldsymbol{\eta}_i = \frac{\Delta Q}{\Delta \mathbf{C}}}

with the understanding that vector division is not mathematically defined in the ordinary scalar sense. It should instead be represented as a quality–cost response vector, or converted into a scalar only after deployment weights are explicitly specified.

32.1 Protocol Specifications

Element Specification
Dataset NQ, MS MARCO, HotpotQA (selected per operation)
Query set size ≥ 1,000 queries per configuration
Query stratification Single-hop vs multi-hop; lexical vs semantic
Retrieval metric Recall@k, nDCG@10
Answer metric EM, F1, INFOF1, CITEF1
Hardware Fixed GPU and CPU configuration per run
Statistical test Paired bootstrap, α = 0.05
Runs ≥ 3 seeds per configuration
Cost measurement Hardware profiler + wall-clock + tokenizer + memory profiler

32.2 First Controlled Retrieval Experiment

For dense RAG:

· Configuration A: $O_7 + O_{24}$
· Configuration B: $O_7 + O_{31} + O_{24}$
· Configuration C: $O_7 + O_{12} + O_{24}$
· Configuration D: $O_7 + O_{30} + O_{24}$
· Configuration E: $O_7 + O_{30} + O_{31} + O_{24}$

The experiment tests whether candidate selection, reranking, and retrieval gating produce independently measurable marginal benefits. The key measurement is not "which system wins?" It is:

\boxed{\text{What quality gain does each added operation buy, at what multidimensional cost?}}

32.3 ITO-Bench Proposal

A benchmark should be constructed with:

· 3 datasets: NQ for $O_5$ vs $O_7$ boundary; HotpotQA for multi-step; MS MARCO for $O_{8d}$ pruning.
· 3 hardware profiles: to demonstrate $H$-dependence of cost.
· 6 cost dimensions: measured with fixed instruments.
· NR table: published explicitly — this alone is a contribution.

---

33. A More General View of ITO

The accumulated evidence suggests that ITO is becoming less like "a better RAG architecture" and more like a formal methodology for discovering computationally sufficient information trajectories.

A conventional architecture asks:

\text{How should we build the system?}

ITO asks:

\boxed{\text{What is the minimum sequence of information transformations required to satisfy the task under explicit constraints?}}

The architecture is then treated as one possible trajectory through the space.

---

34. Current ITO Principle

The emerging principle can be stated compactly:

\boxed{\text{Do not optimise architectures as monoliths. Decompose them into measurable operations, measure conditional contribution and multidimensional cost, then optimise trajectories.}}

Or formally:

\boxed{\mathcal{P}^\star(q) = \left\{ p \in \mathcal{P}(q) \;\middle|\; \mathbf{Q}(p) \succeq \boldsymbol{\tau}, \;\nexists p' : \mathbf{C}(p') \preceq \mathbf{C}(p) \right\}}

The critical object is therefore not the architecture $A$ itself, but the trajectory:

p : S_0 \rightarrow S_1 \rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow S_n.

