Appendix B: Cost Composition Rules

This appendix specifies the per-dimension composition rules for the operator  \oplus  introduced in §4.3. The rules are constructed from validated literature on RAG cost accounting.

B.1 Formal Definition

Let a trajectory be  p = (o_1, \ldots, o_n) . The trajectory cost is:

\mathbf{C}(p) = \bigoplus_{o_i \in p} \mathbf{C}_i(\theta_i, D, H)

where the composition operator  \oplus  is instantiated per dimension as follows:

B.2 Per-Dimension Composition

Dimension Composition rule Rationale
 C_{\text{latency}}   \max_i C_{\text{latency}}(o_i)  under pipelining;  \sum_i C_{\text{latency}}(o_i)  under sequential execution Parallelizable stages (e.g., embedding) achieve  3\times  cost reductions, whereas latency-sensitive inference remains constrained by network variability
 C_{\text{memory}}   \max_i C_{\text{memory}}(o_i)  under shared pool;  \sum_i C_{\text{memory}}(o_i)  under isolated execution HNSW indexes must reside fully in memory to operate; shared memory pools are common in GPU deployments
 C_{\text{tokens}}   \sum_i C_{\text{tokens}}(o_i)  Token consumption is additive by definition
 C_{\text{calls}}   \sum_i C_{\text{calls}}(o_i)  Discrete invocation events accumulate
 C_{\text{compute}}  Model-dependent (e.g., Roofline model) Compute cost depends on arithmetic intensity and memory bandwidth
 C_{\text{network}}  Model-dependent Network cost depends on request size, protocol, and topology

B.3 Offline/Online Amortization

The effective cost of a trajectory with offline component  C_{\text{offline}}  and online component  C_{\text{online}}  over  Q  queries is:

C_{\text{eff}}(Q) = \frac{C_{\text{offline}}}{Q} + C_{\text{online}}

As  Q \to \infty , the amortized offline term decreases monotonically and the effective cost approaches the online retrieval cost. This formalizes the write-once, read-many behaviour of RAG systems.

B.4 Non-Additivity Sources

The additive form  \sum  is a first-order approximation valid when operations are resource-independent and sequentially executed. Non-additivity arises from:

1. Shared memory: Multiple operations using the same GPU memory pool.
2. Pipelined latency: Retrieval and generation can overlap.
3. Caching: Repeated calls to the same operation may be cheaper.
4. Fixed overhead: Per-call overhead does not scale linearly.
5. Incremental updates: For dynamic knowledge bases, incremental maintenance scales as  O(\Delta N)  whereas full rebuilding scales as  O(N + \Delta N) .

